-- title:
--   Proposition 4 — if $C_T\succeq C^M_T$, the newsvendor optimum dominates the risk-neutral optimum
-- statement:
--   Throughout, the standing model of §3.1 holds: $B,\tilde B$ independent Brownian motions with augmented filtrations $\mathcal F\supseteq\mathcal G$, the asset $dX_t=X_t(\mu_t\,dt+\sigma_t\,dB_t)$, the demand component $C$ with $A_T=C_T+\tilde\sigma\tilde B_T$, the two Markov hypotheses, and $\mathbb P^M$ with $d\mathbb P^M/d\mathbb P=Z_T$ (12).
--
--   Suppose Assumptions 1–5 hold (Assumption 1 for the law of $A_T$ under $\mathbb P$ and under $\mathbb P^M$), let $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ be the solution of problem (2) (a maximizer of $\mathbb E[H_T]$ over $D$ at which Assumptions 2–3 hold), and suppose $\mathbb E^M[(C_T)^-]<\infty$. If $C_T\succeq C^M_T$, then
--
--   1. every maximizer $(P^{NV(M)},R^{NV(M)})$ of $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$ over $D$ satisfies
--   $$
--   P^{NV}\ge P^{NV(M)},\qquad R^{NV}\ge R^{NV(M)};
--   $$
--   2. $\mathbb E[H_T(P,R)]\ge V_0(P,R)$ for all $(P,R)$ with $P\ge s$, in particular on $D$.
--
--   Together with Proposition 5 this gives Theorem 2.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: $\mathcal F_t$ and $\mathcal G_t$ are the filtrations generated by $(B_t,\tilde B_t)$ and $B_t$ (p.11) → the $\mathbb P$-augmented natural filtrations of $(B,\tilde B)$ and of $B$; reason: martingale representation (Prop. EC.3) holds for the augmented Brownian filtration, and null-set modifications of an adapted process must stay adapted
--   - reading: $(X_t,\mu_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P$ and $(X_t,\sigma_t)$ is Markovian under $\mathbb P^M$ (pp.11–12) → the simple Markov property on $[0,T]$ with respect to $\mathcal F$: $\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\mathcal F_s]=\mathbb E_Q[g(Y_t)\mid\sigma(Y_s)]$ a.s. for $s\le t\le T$ and bounded measurable $g$; reason: the paper does not define the term; this is the standard definition, relative to the filtration of footnote 4
--   - reading: $c$ the unit production cost, $s$ the unit salvage value, $X_0$ an asset price → $s<c$ and $x_0>0$ in the parameter structure; reason: the newsvendor convention (p.7), implied by Assumption 3 wherever it is assumed; $X_t>0$ is used on p.ec28
--   - encoding: processes on $[0,T]$ → processes indexed by $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$, constrained only for $t\le T$; reason: Mathlib's Brownian motion lives on $\mathbb R_{\ge0}$; values after $T$ never enter
--   - encoding: stochastic integrals $\int\theta\,dX$, $\int b\,d\tilde B$, $\int\eta\,dB$ with almost-sure integrability → the published relation `EthierKurtz.HasBrownianItoIntegral` (integrand cut off after $T$, square integrable on every path) plus a pathwise $du$-integral of the drift on every path; reason: under the augmented filtration an integrand can be set to zero on the exceptional null set without losing measurability, so the classes agree up to indistinguishability
--   - reading: for $u\le v$, $C_v-C_u$ is adapted to the σ-algebra generated by $\{X_t,u\le t\le v\}$ (p.11) → $C_v-C_u$ is measurable with respect to $\sigma\{X_t:u\le t\le v\}$, for $u\le v\le T$; reason: "adapted" for a single random variable means measurable
--   - encoding: $Z_t=e^{-\int_0^t\eta_s dB_s-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_s^2ds}$ (12) → $Z_t=\exp(-I_t-\frac12\int_0^t\eta_u^2du)$ with $I$ a $\mathcal G$-adapted version of the Itô integral $\int\eta\,dB$; reason: an Itô integral is fixed only up to a null set; the paper uses that $Z$ is $\mathcal G$-adapted (p.ec23)
--   - encoding: $C_T\succeq C^M_T$: $\mathbb P(C^M_T\le a)=\mathbb P^M(C_T\le a)\ge\mathbb P(C_T\le a)$ for all $a$ (pp.12–13) → `StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder P^M P C_T C_T`: $\mathbb P^M(C_T>x)\le\mathbb P(C_T>x)$ for all $x$; reason: $C^M_T$ enters only through its law; for probability measures the tail form is the distribution-function form
--   - reading: $(P^{NV},R^{NV})$ the solution of problem (2), named by Assumptions 2–3 → any maximizer of $\mathbb E[H_T]$ over $D$ at which Assumptions 2 and 3 hold; reason: uniqueness is the content of Proposition 1 and is not built into a definition
--   - reading: $(P^{NV(M)},R^{NV(M)}):=\arg\max_{P,R}V_0(P,R)$ (p.21) → every maximizer of $V_0$ over $D$; reason: the stronger reading; Assumption 2 is not imposed under $\mathbb P^M$, so uniqueness is not assumed
--   - reading: (ii) for all $(P,R)$ → for all $(P,R)$ with $P\ge s$, a superset of $D$; reason: for $P<s$ the payoff decreases in the market size and the inequality reverses
--   - encoding: $V_0(P,R)=\mathbb E^M[H_T(P,R)]$, presupposed finite → the hypothesis $\mathbb E^M[(C_T)^-]<\infty$; reason: otherwise the Bochner integral is a default $0$; under $\mathbb P$ integrability already follows from Assumption 2
--   - reading: arg max / arg min over $(P,R)$ → over the decision domain $D=\{P\ge c,\ R-bP\ge0\}$ of (2); reason: p.9: the constraints are non-binding and "the same applies to all relevant settings throughout the paper"
--   - encoding: "martingale" (relative to $\mathcal F_t$, footnote 4) → `IsMartingaleOn`: adapted, integrable, and the martingale identity for $s\le t\le T$; reason: the paper's martingales live on $[0,T]$; Mathlib's `Martingale` constrains all times
-- source:
--   Wang–Yao–Zhang, How Does Risk Hedging Impact Operations? Insights from a Price-Setting Newsvendor Model, arXiv:2201.01026v4, Proposition 4, p.21; proof in §EC.8.1, pp.ec47–ec48

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_HedgePSNV_Model
import Definitions.Def_StochasticOrders_Usual_UsualOrder

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace HedgePSNV

/-- Proposition 4, arXiv:2201.01026v4, p.21: suppose Assumptions 1–5
hold and `C_T ⪰ C^M_T`. Let `(P^NV, R^NV)` be the solution of problem (2) (a maximizer of
`E[H_T(P,R)]` over the decision domain satisfying Assumption 2, and Assumption 3 holds). Then
(i) every maximizer `(P^NV(M), R^NV(M))` of `V_0(P,R) = E^M[H_T(P,R)]` over the decision
domain satisfies `P^NV ≥ P^NV(M)` and `R^NV ≥ R^NV(M)`; (ii) `E[H_T(P,R)] ≥ V_0(P,R)` for all
`(P, R)` with `P ≥ s` (in particular on the decision domain, where `P ≥ c > s`).

**Formalization Note** `C_T ⪰ C^M_T` is `UsualOrder P^M P C_T C_T` (the law of `C_T` under
`P^M` is below its law under `P`). Assumption 1 is imposed on the law of `A_T` under `P` and
under `P^M`. "For all (P,R)" in (ii) is read over the decision domain (p.9) and stated on the
larger set `P ≥ s`; for `P < s` the payoff decreases in the market size and the inequality
reverses.
`NegPartIntegrable` under `P^M` states that `V_0(P,R) = E^M[H_T(P,R)]` exists; under `P` the
integrability is already forced by `E[H_T(P^NV,R^NV)] > 0` in Assumption 2. -/
theorem proposition_4 (p : Params) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (M : Model p Ω)
    (hM : M.Standing) (hintM : M.NegPartIntegrable M.PM) (h4 : M.Assumption4) (h5 : M.Assumption5)
    (h1P : Assumption1 (M.P.map M.AT)) (h1M : Assumption1 (M.PM.map M.AT))
    (PNV RNV : ℝ) (hNV : IsNVMaximizer p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (h2 : Assumption2 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV) (h3 : Assumption3 p M.P M.AT PNV RNV)
    (hpos : StochasticOrders.Usual.UsualOrder M.PM M.P (M.C p.T) (M.C p.T)) :
    (∀ P' R' : ℝ, IsNVMaximizer p M.PM M.AT P' R' → P' ≤ PNV ∧ R' ≤ RNV) ∧
    ∀ P R : ℝ, p.s ≤ P → M.V0 P R ≤ nvValue p M.P M.AT P R := by sorry

end HedgePSNV
